 and industrialized it he embarked  on a type of rule that involved regular  mass terror and yet that same country  achieved a victory a military victory  over Nazi Germany that would have been  inconceivable without the Soviet  participation at the end of the Second  World War the USSR was one of the two  superpowers that dominated the globe and  Stalin's USSR became a model for States  in Eastern Europe and for the People's  Republic of China and one man dominated  Soviet politics in in that period and  that man was Joseph Stalin so it's easy  to see why people have written or been  attracted to writing biographies of  Joseph Stalin those biographies follower  a conventional pattern the usual story  is that Stalin was a mere bureaucrat  that he had no ideas no talent was a  singularly uncultured man that he wasn't  really a believer in Marxism Leninism  that he was pathologically devoted only  to the aggrandizement of his own power  that he had a sort of split life a  bureaucrat in the first half of his life  who turned out in the second half of his  life to be a pathological killer that's  one thing I think that the biographies  haven't really previously tackled I  thought that it was worth having a go to  another biography not just because  archives have now opened up that were  previously closed to us and not just  because now we can go more easily to  Abkhazia where he he had his holidays or  to Georgia where he spent his childhood  or to parts of even Moscow that were  previously forbidden to us I thought it  was worth having another attempt at a  new portrait of this man because I  thought that most of the conventional  picture was unconvincing so I started  with the idea that it would be good to  use the new archives it would be good to  visit the bits of the old Soviet Union  that Stalin had been to and lived in but  also because I thought a new image of  the man a new picture of the man that  needed to be written  usually biographies skip over the first  