mpire  his homeland became bigger as his  Marxism became more important to him so  he started off as a Georgian patriot and  became a Marxist internationalist in the  Russian Empire with a commitment to  making Russian Russia the first place to  have the big socialist revolution the  importance of Stalin then was disguised  by the fact that he never became an  émigré that he was content to be a  clandestine militant but he rose to  prominence in the 1917 revolution he was  one of them the most important of  Lenin's left tenants and he ran the  Central Committee of the Bolshevik Party  when Lenin had to go into hiding one of  the things I think I found most  fascinating was that he had a poncho on  for terror he revealed this portion or  for terror in the civil war that  followed the October Revolution of 1917  he was notorious for the wasteful way he  conducted military strategy thousands of  peasants died unnecessarily in the Red  Army and thousands of peasants perished  because of the terror actions that he  directed against villages that were  thought to sympathize with the anti red  side he put officers on a barge officers  who were fighting in the Red Army for  the Red Army in 1918 because he hated  anything to do with the old Tsarist  order now this was against party policy  so he had this idea of surgically  removing elements social elements from  society even more excessive an urge than  his fellow Bolsheviks had so the idea  that there were two Stalin's there was a  sort of Starlin the bureaucrat up to the  end of the 1920s  and then Stalin the pathological killer  in the 1930s really doesn't stand up to  close scrutiny the other thing that came  across to me in the course of the  research was it was quite a surprise to  me really was how charismatic he was  what a great team leader he was how easy  it was for him to make himself appealing  to a sort of laddish rough crude element  inside the Bolshevik Party there really  was a Stalin team kaganovich Molotov and  several others were already recognizing  him as their leader well before he  became the dominant figure in Soviet  politics and when Lenin left his  testament saying that there would be two  men who would card in early fight for  the succession one would be Trotsky  everybody expected that the other would  be Stalin no one expected that but if  you look at the records Lenin wasn't  saying anything that already a lot of  people felt he was already a dynamic  leader not just a chief bureaucrat  inside the party and the memoirs that  Dan played this were memoirs by people  who had been beaten by Stalin and they  they they went out of their way to  portray sides of him that put them in  the good light rather than Stalin there  was something of the leader and not just  the chief bureaucrat already in the  1920s the other thing that I think needs  to be adjusted in our picture of Stalin  is that we think of him as being  irrational now I did go around  to several psychiatrists in writing this  book I have to say it gets to you it's a  it's a very gruesome  it does actually get to you but the  reason I went to psychiatrists was  because I was bothered by the number of  biographers who said that Stalin was mad  and it seemed to me that this was worth  looking at because it's a bit of a  cop-out if he was mad  then it more or less winds up all need  for explanation he was a pathological  killer psychiatrists tended to when I  told them the details of what I knew  about his psychology tended to confirm  that he would not now be called mad by  psychiatrists actually they don't use  the word mad they use the word psychotic  he would not be called psychotic what he  had was a gross personality disorder and  that means that he doesn't get off with  what he did it means that he had a  maladjusted focus on the way he saw the  world around him and what I tried to  bring out in this biography is that the  world around him was dangerous to him to  his party and to the Soviet state there  were millions of people who hated Stalin  he had some reason to move against them  to repress them if you had the kind of  ideology the kind of policies that he  had there were millions of peasants who  had been pushed into collective farms  who hated him there were hundreds of  thousands of members of other political  parties who had been suppressed and  thrown into the gulag  there were tens of thousands of priests  thousands of rabbis thousands of mullahs  and Imams who had landed up in  concentration camps  and all of their families didn't contain  very many admirers  of Joseph Stalin so when he launched the  Great Terror and in the end of the 1930s  far from it being a totally mad scheme  of repression it was a hugely excessive  reaction by a maladjusted leader to a  real existing genuine atmosphere of  hostility to him so the history of the  Great Terror really does need rewriting  because of the way that the state was  organized however he couldn't get the  victims that he wanted he told people to  go out for the the priests the mullahs  the the former Mensheviks the former  socialist revolutionaries the former  Trotskyites and so on the state system  however was not like Nazi Germany it was  chaotic and the people who were told to  arrest numbers of people and they were  given specific numbers of people to  arrest they just arrested up to that  number and they didn't worry too much  about the categories so that the history  of the great terror and the and the  career of Stalin are much more complex  than I think most of the biographies  have so far allowed there was something  very peculiar about this man  lots of people face lots of politicians  in history have faced pervasive popular  hatred and yet have not turned to the  methods that Stalin used so I'm not in  any way downplaying his peculiar  peculiarity he had a vengeful side that  we all know about  he lived in a marriage that was bound to  make him more edgy than it would  otherwise have been his wife had all  manner of mental problems he treated her  badly so he made those problems worse  so the marriage became worse the  marriage became more jumpy and less  congenial and his wife eventually could  take no more and killed herself and  after that he turned in on himself  became more and more distrustful of  everyone around him so this man who  already had a gross personality disorder  hid conditions that made him more edgy  more suspicious more vengeful and made  the terror worse than it probably would  even have been he was however a believer  the thing that really took me while  doing the research and looking at his  books he was a he was a real peasant he  took notes on everything he read he was  also a bit of a delinquent so he marked  all the books that he read and he wrote  his reactions in the margins so he  didn't write book reviews but he wrote  in the margins of the books and you can  see the care with which he read Lenin  Marx angles it's inconceivable that this  man didn't really believe this stuff so  that pictures of him azan someone who  turned into a world statesman who just  pragmatically adjusted himself to  whatever the current deals were that he  could do with Roosevelt or Truman or  Churchill or aptly or or motsek tongue  they miss part of the point he did do  those deals he did think pragmatically  but underpinning everything else was an  ideological commitment to Marxism  Leninism or as his own propagandists  called it Marxism Leninism Stalinism he  himself knew that this cult was  ludicrous this cult of him  the leader was ludicrous he periodically  said look don't issue so many books  about me don't don't put up so many  posters of me and he hath believed it he  hath believed that it had gone too far  but the people who were organizing the  cult around him they knew that if they  didn't issue those books if they didn't  pin up those posters they'd be for the  high jump so it was a complicated  psychological game that was going on  inside Stalin's head and a ruthless  political interplay that was going on in  the broader world of social politic of  Soviet politics he ruled in a way that  was always going to be despotic once he  got power  he totally identified himself with the  October Revolution as its Savior as the  new Lenin he maintained his power by a  totalitarian system that imposed a  vertical chain of command from the party  through the public institutions of the  army and the government right down to  the smallest little agencies of  statehood but that power was exercised  only for periods in a regular  predictable way because in order to to  control the Red Army to control the  Communist Party to control these great  economic ministries he needed to keep  all the fellow rulers his subordinates  on their toes so at the same time as  introducing totalitarian regularity and  uniformity he maintained total  unpredictability utter chaos he didn't  want anybody near him to think that they  had a steady job  he arrested the wives of some of the  Politburo members he shot the brother of  kaganovich he arrested the wife of  Molotov none of them knew whether at the  end of the Working Day they were going  to be at home in their dachas or or  inside the Lubyanka prison in central  Moscow being tortured to confess to  imaginary crimes so this was a very very  idiosyncratic murderous system the last  question that I wanted to deal with in  the biography and it was a question that  had intrigued me for many years was how  did this system relate to the way the  Soviet Union was ruled lower down the  system outside the Kremlin and what I  found there which confirmed a lot of  what I'd been thinking about in writing  earlier books on the Soviet Union was  that he didn't have total control I  don't think any ruler can have total  control but Stalin was so so vengeful so  murderous so brutal in the way that he  intruded in the public life of the  Soviet Union that it was natural for his  subordinates especially those who  weren't in his sight to find all sorts  of ways of wriggling out of doing  exactly what he wanted it was a  self-protection mechanism if they wanted  to survive they couldn't do everything  he wanted all of the time  so they chose the things that they would  do they picked and they chose and they  hope they chose correctly there were  little systems of patron and boss  sorry patron and client which protected  people in the localities little nests of  of institutional groupings whereby  people survived this manic system so  Stalin knew that this was going on he  knew that he could he knew that he  couldn't really even trust all of the  information coming up to him this was  not a perfect system of information it  was not a perfect system therefore it  couldn't be if you don't get perfect  information when you're running an  economy that big without any private  sector at all then you can't have  perfect commands so that he he tried to  perfect it one of the reasons he  launched the Great Terror was to get rid  of perceived enemies another was to  perfect the totalitarian system he found  that he couldn't he found that he was  just wiping out generation after  generation of officials and the system  stayed the same so he became ironically  in the end a communist conservative he  put up with the 90% of the results that  he wanted and he settled for that he  never got total control but for people  near him that didn't make any difference  they were terrified of him people very  near to him also respected him even  admired him so the Molotov wept when he  died  Molotov whose wife whom he dearly loved  was in prison and about to be tortured  and him involved in an anti-semitic  trial yet that same person Molotov  deeply deeply mourned his passing this  man Stalin was I wouldn't say he's in it  and enigma  there wouldn't be any point in writing  the book if he still thought there was a  mystery but he was complex he was  certainly not the unimpressive  uncultured bureaucrat of the  conventional biographies and that's why  I thought it was worth writing as bigger  a doorstep as I've written it's always  useful to throw around when you're in a  an angry mood or prop open a door but I  hope it has more purposes than that  thank you  yeah I'll be delighted  thanks all all of you for coming  Roberts services author of Lenin a  biography and a history of modern Russia  his latest Stalin of biography is  published by Harvard University Press  visit hup harvard.edu

