nates especially those who  weren't in his sight to find all sorts  of ways of wriggling out of doing  exactly what he wanted it was a  self-protection mechanism if they wanted  to survive they couldn't do everything  he wanted all of the time  so they chose the things that they would  do they picked and they chose and they  hope they chose correctly there were  little systems of patron and boss  sorry patron and client which protected  people in the localities little nests of  of institutional groupings whereby  people survived this manic system so  Stalin knew that this was going on he  knew that he could he knew that he  couldn't really even trust all of the  information coming up to him this was  not a perfect system of information it  was not a perfect system therefore it  couldn't be if you don't get perfect  information when you're running an  economy that big without any private  sector at all then you can't have  perfect commands so that he he tried to  perfect it one of the reasons he  launched the Great Terror was to get rid  of perceived enemies another was to  perfect the totalitarian system he found  that he couldn't he found that he was  just wiping out generation after  generation of officials and the system  stayed the same so he became ironically  in the end a communist conservative he  put up with the 90% of the results that  he wanted and he settled for that he  never got total control but for people  near him that didn't make any difference  they were terrified of him people very  near to him also respected him even  admired him so the Molotov wept when he  died  Molotov whose wife whom he dearly loved  was in prison and about to be tortured  and him involved in an anti-semitic  trial yet that same person Molotov  deeply deeply mourned his passing this  man Stalin was I wouldn't say he's in it  and enigma  there wouldn't be any point in writing  the book if he still thought there was a  mystery but he was complex he was  certainly not the unimpressive  uncultured bureaucrat of the  conventional biographies and that's why  I thought it was worth writing as bigger  a doorstep as I've written it's always  useful to throw around when you're in a  an angry mood or prop open a door but I  hope it has more purposes than that  thank you  yeah I'll be delighted  thanks all all of you for coming  Roberts services author of Lenin a  biography and a history of modern Russia  his latest Stalin of biography is  published by Harvard University Press  visit hup harvard.edu
 

